-- ================================================================
-- guardians.llm — THE PRIMITIVE. One call: text in, text out.
--
-- This is what an LLM API actually is, and it is the example's ONLY
-- task-agnostic model surface. `summarize` and `observe` are ordinary anthill
-- operations built on top (tasks.anthill), not separate host functions — a
-- real integration binds one HTTP call, not one function per task.
-- ================================================================

-- A prompt is a value carrying a trust label
enum guardians.Prompt
  import guardians.{Text}
  sort Trust = ?
  entity prompt(body: Text[Trust])
end

-- WHAT A MODEL RETURNS IS ORDINARY UNTRUSTED TEXT, and it used to be a sealed
-- `LlmOutput` with a `Permission[Reveal]`-gated `text_of` beside it. Both are
-- gone (WI-20260829-MCKTE), and what removed them is that the general mechanism
-- grew strong enough to subsume the special one.
--
-- THE OLD JUSTIFICATION WAS "A TRUST LABEL IS STRIPPABLE, AN OPAQUE WRAPPER IS
-- NOT", and it was true when written: `text(raw: u.raw)` re-labelled anything in
-- one line, so a label could not carry the weight and an opaque wrapper had to.
-- Since `Text`'s constructor became `internal` and its `raw` projection with it,
-- NO text's content can reach a `String` at all, and no `Text[Trusted]` can be
-- minted without `Permission[Vouch]`. A model's reply is confined by exactly the
-- same two facts that confine a mailbox body — so the wrapper was restating the
-- label's own guarantee in a second vocabulary.
--
-- AND `text_of` HAD NO CALLER OUTSIDE A REFUSED PROGRAM. Its one use in the
-- whole example was the checker fixture that consulted a model, which is the pattern
-- docs/design/measured.md warns about — a vocabulary appearing only in refused
-- programs is indistinguishable from one that refuses everything — and the same
-- finding that deleted `widen`/`declassify` from the vocabulary.
--
-- WHAT IS GIVEN UP, stated because it is real: a `Text`-valued field added to
-- `CheckResult` later would open a channel `Permission[Reveal]` would have caught
-- and the label alone does not, an `Untrusted` diagnostic being unremarkable. That
-- is a specific future guard traded for one less mechanism, and it is the trade
-- this example already made once.

sort guardians.Llm
  import anthill.prelude.{Error}
  import guardians.{Prompt, Text}
  import guardians.TrustLevel.{Untrusted}
  import guardians.TrustLevel.{Untrusted}

  sort C = ?
  -- WI-320 / proposal 045, the `Stream` shape: `External` is a fact about a
  -- CARRIER, not about being an `Llm`, so the row is a parameter each carrier
  -- instantiates. Welded on here it was a claim about every implementation that
  -- only `LiveLlm` could honour — and it made `Branch` unusable with ANY model,
  -- including the fixture, because 054's `Branch × External` exclusion fires on
  -- the declared row and cannot see which carrier is behind it.
  effects E = ?

  operation complete(self: C, p: Prompt) -> Text[Untrusted]
    effects {E, Error}
end

-- ================================================================
-- THE CARRIERS ARE CAPABILITY OBJECTS (proposal 064).
--
-- Each constructor is `internal`, which kernel-language.md §8.6 makes the ONLY
-- hide gate: it hides the name from cross-scope resolution AND from field
-- projection, with top-level code outside every declaring scope (WI-977). So
-- `open` is the only way to obtain an `Llm`, and `open` carries
-- `Permission[Llm]` — the runtime consultation of an ambient grant, which can
-- refuse.
--
-- WITHOUT `internal` THE EFFECT IS ADVISORY, and that is not a slogan here: it
-- is what these two lines used to be. Before 064 a generated checker could write
-- `fake_llm(fixture: "x")` and hold a model, and construction carried no effect
-- at all, so nothing caught it at the moment of ACQUISITION. `internal` is what
-- makes the mint the only door, and `rejected/forged_llm.anthill` measures it.
--
-- `complete` CARRIES NO `Permission`, which is 064 holding: the label goes on
-- the mint and nowhere else. The check already happened, and the `Llm` in this
-- signature is the evidence of it.
--
-- IT USED TO CARRY A SECOND LABEL, `Model`, answering a DIFFERENT question —
-- *did this code consult a model*, not *did it acquire the authority to*. That
-- question stopped being worth asking, and it is the ANSWER that changed rather
-- than the question: a component that calls a model learns nothing from the call,
-- because the reply is a `Text` whose content nothing can read. So of the three
-- ways a checker can reach a model, only two are failures:
--
--   holds an Llm it was handed, calls it   HARMLESS — the reply is unreadable, so
--                                          it teaches nothing, and READING it is
--                                          the offence (rejected/reads_text)
--   holds none, mints one                  caught by `-Permission[Llm]` (minting_checker)
--   holds none, forges one                 caught by `internal`         (forged_llm)
--
-- CONSULTING TAKES NO AUTHORITY, and a draft of this file claimed it did. Building
-- a prompt is label-PRESERVING (`entity prompt(body: Text[Trust])`) and `complete`
-- takes an unparameterized `Prompt` on purpose, since `summarize` sends untrusted
-- mailbox content to a model. `fixtures/agent/consulting_checker.anthill` is the
-- control that loads. What the pipeline's own shape confines is narrower: a checker
-- holding a SPEC-typed `Llm` is refused by `undeclared effect: ?E`, the carrier row
-- `Checker.check` has no slot for — the effect row, not the prompt.
-- ================================================================
sort guardians.LiveLlm
  import anthill.prelude.{String, Error, External, Permission}
  import guardians.{Llm, Prompt, Text}
  import guardians.TrustLevel.{Untrusted}

  internal entity live_llm(endpoint: String, model: String)

  -- THE MINT, and the sole introduction. The `Permission[Llm]` is consumed
  -- here and nowhere downstream.
  operation open(endpoint: String, model: String) -> LiveLlm
    effects {Permission[Llm]} = live_llm(endpoint: endpoint, model: model)

  -- A NARROWER CAPABILITY FOR THE SAME OBJECT, which is what makes it a probe
  -- rather than a gate: `open` above mints the identical `LiveLlm` under the
  -- weaker `Permission[Llm]`, so this demands more and protects nothing. What it
  -- measures is the DOWNWARD CLOSURE — `LiveLlm provides Llm`, so `-Permission[Llm]`
  -- forbids this one too and a denial cannot be evaded by naming a narrower
  -- capability (rejected/frontier_checker.anthill). Note the closure runs one way
  -- only: `-Permission[LiveLlm]` would NOT forbid `open`.
  operation open_frontier(endpoint: String) -> LiveLlm
    effects {Permission[LiveLlm]} =
      live_llm(endpoint: endpoint, model: "frontier")

  operation complete(self: LiveLlm, p: Prompt) -> Text[Untrusted]
    effects {External, Error}
  provides Llm[E = {External}]
end

sort guardians.FakeLlm
  import anthill.prelude.{String, Error, Permission}
  import guardians.{Llm, Prompt, Text}
  import guardians.TrustLevel.{Untrusted}

  internal entity fake_llm(fixture: String)

  -- THE TEST DOUBLE OCCUPIES 064's TOP-RIGHT CELL: `Permission` without
  -- `External`. The same authority path is exercised — the grant is consulted
  -- and may refuse — and nothing leaves the process. That the two axes are
  -- orthogonal is what makes a double possible without a second code path.
  operation open(fixture: String) -> FakeLlm
    effects {Permission[Llm]} = fake_llm(fixture: fixture)

  operation complete(self: FakeLlm, p: Prompt) -> Text[Untrusted]
    effects {Error}
  provides Llm[E = {}]
end

namespace guardians
  import anthill.prelude.{String, List}
  import guardians.{Text, Prompt, LiveLlm, FakeLlm}
  import guardians.TrustLevel.{Trusted}

  -- THE LATTICE JOIN, and the one place a label is DECIDED. `Trusted` is bottom,
  -- so `Trusted ⊔ ?t = ?t` and the variable carries the answer with no ordering
  -- machinery. An n-point lattice would need the general join; variance IS
  -- declarable and would give it (measured: docs/design/measured.md C4).
  operation prompt_with(instruction : Text[Trusted],
                        content     : Text[?t]) -> Prompt[?t]

  -- CONCATENATION IS UNTRUSTED-ONLY, AND IT USED TO BE LABEL-POLYMORPHIC
  -- (`List[T = Text[?t]] -> Text[?t]`, "preserves the label of its parts"). That
  -- was correct for every non-empty list and WRONG at `nil`: with no parts there is
  -- no label to preserve, so preservation is vacuous the way `all([])` is true, `?t`
  -- instantiates to whatever the sink asks, and `join_texts(nil)` was a
  -- `Text[Trusted]` obtained with neither `Permission[Vouch]` nor the `approved`
  -- obligation. Measured: it reached `Email.send`'s body, and the PREVIOUS design
  -- refused it — `requires releasable(body)` on the sink caught the term — so moving
  -- the content tier to the mint (WI-20260829-MCKTE) lost a check until this line.
  --
  -- THE NARROWING COSTS NOTHING because every caller joins mailbox content:
  -- `summarize` (tasks.anthill) and the generation attack in
  -- rejected/generate_from_content.anthill are the only ones. A trusted-text
  -- concatenation is a reasonable operation and can come back the day something
  -- needs it — with a caller, and with the `nil` case decided rather than inferred.
  operation join_texts(parts: List[T = Text[Untrusted]]) -> Text[Untrusted]

  -- ONE host function for the whole model surface, keyed per carrier.
  provides LiveLlm language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-core/tests/guardians_test.rs"
    operation_map { complete: "guardians_live_complete" }
  end

  provides FakeLlm language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-core/tests/guardians_test.rs"
    operation_map { complete: "guardians_fake_complete" }
  end
end
